    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d340">

                <pb facs="0530" n="420" sameAs="frus:frus1928v03/pg420"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/5251: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Munro</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-01-13T09:00:00-05:45"><hi rendition="#italic">January 13,
                                1928—9 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 12:10
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>23. Antonio Medrano, one of the substitute judges of the Supreme Court, has been
                    selected as the Liberal Vice Presidential candidate on the ticket with
                        Moncada.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d340-f1"/> His friends have asked me to
                    ascertain whether the Department sees any reason to question the
                    constitutionality of his election. Apparently article 126<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d340-f2"/> is the only one which could possibly bear on the
                    question.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Munro</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
